I've written the plan for two tasks. The commit step didn't record anything because session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git refused the add (the plan's command ends in || true for this case). The files are on disk:

• session_plan/task_01.md
• session_plan/task_02.md
• session_plan/issue_responses.md

Task 1 (product, Fixes #1103, filed at planning). If you mistype the provider (for example --provider anthropc) and give no base URL, yoyo should refuse at startup with exit 2 and suggest the closest provider name. Today it does this instead:
• prints the same warning 5 times
• spends about 80 seconds retrying against localhost:8080
• sends that server a real API key from the fallback chain
• tells you to check your internet connection and use /retry, which only exists inside the interactive session

The fix applies to both the command-line flag and .yoyo.toml. An unknown name with a base URL keeps working as now, since the user pointed it somewhere on purpose. The /provider command inside a session gets the same refusal if that fits within three files. Otherwise the task is titled "Part of #1103" and the gap goes on the ticket.

Task 2 (product, backlog drain, Fixes #1066). With a deny list set, eval "$(...)" stays refused except for a short fixed list of setup tools whose output is shell setup, such as ssh-agent, direnv hook and brew shellenv. The exception only applies when the inner command is a single plain call by bare name and passes the deny check itself. The task file lists the test cases that must stay refused, answers the issue's two objections, and says to update the refusal message, which currently names ssh-agent as refused.

Ticket and release notes:
• #1086 now has a comment: two more commands print the in-session / form when run from the shell (yoyo docs and yoyo ast with no arguments), and two commands exit 0 after failing (yoyo ast without ast-grep, yoyo goal set with no description).
• #991 stays on hold: @yuanhao hasn't answered my either/or yet.
• No release this session. One isn't due by the cadence, and I'd rather ship after these two fixes land.
